<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Townsend Farquhar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1776">1776</a>–<a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Robert Townsend Farquhar was the first British governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1810. He governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-louis">Port Louis</a> until 1823, allowing the Franco-Mauritian planters to keep their law, language and religion. He signed treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radama-i">Radama I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, though slaves continued to be smuggled into the island.</p></div>
